/-!
# A two-generator upper bound for level-four parabolic cohomology

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.parabolicH1_finrank_add_one_le_level_four`
(`da582f0e-0cd3-4ccf-80a2-7b84eee0aa59`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `c1a440ec-3b18-4801-8ef1-898f57bee8ad`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $n>0$ be an integer, and let $H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(\Gamma_1(4),\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathbf C^2)$
denote the quotient of parabolic one-cocycles by principal cocycles, with the binary-form action
used in the MTT mission. Then

$$\dim_{\mathbf C} H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(\Gamma_1(4),\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathbf C^2)+1\le n.$$

The estimate holds in both parities. In odd modular weight $k=n+2$, it supplies the cohomological
half of the remaining level-four dimension comparison.

## Explanation of the source proof

# A two-generator bound at level four

Write (V_n=\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathbf C^2), with the left binary-form
action fixed by the MTT definitions. We prove, for every (n>0),

$$\dim_{\mathbf C}H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(\Gamma_1(4),V_n)+1\le n.$$

Let (T=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right))
and (U=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&0\\-4&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)).
The first ingredient is that (T,U) generate \(\Gamma_1(4)\).
We establish this directly by Schreier's lemma. A right transversal for
\(\Gamma_0(4)\) in \(SL_2(\mathbf Z)\) is

$$1,\ S,\ ST,\ ST^2,\ ST^3,\
\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\2&1\end{pmatrix}.$$

The bottom row modulo four determines the representative: its first entry
zero selects the identity; first entry two selects the last representative;
otherwise the product of its two entries determines one of the four middle
representatives. The determinant-one condition makes this assignment unique.
The proof certifies that finite modular calculation and all twelve Schreier
generators in Lean's kernel. Each generator is a word in (T,U,-I), so these
three elements generate \(\Gamma_0(4)\). The subgroup generated by (T,U)
lies in \(\Gamma_1(4)\), which does not contain \(-I\). Removing the disjoint
central sign therefore gives the asserted two-generator description.

Now take a parabolic cocycle normalized by (c(T)=0). Its value at (U)
determines the entire cocycle: the cocycle identity propagates equality from
the two generators to products and inverses. Since (U) fixes a rational
cusp, parabolicity places (c(U)) in \(\operatorname{im}(\rho(U)-1)\).
Consequently the normalized cocycle space injects linearly into that image.
The nonzero vector (Y^n) is fixed by (U), so rank-nullity, together with
\(\dim V_n=n+1\), gives

$$\dim\operatorname{im}(\rho(U)-1)\le n.$$

Finally the previously proved normalization-kernel theorem identifies the
normalized cocycle dimension with \(\dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}+1\), proving
the estimate. No parity hypothesis is used. In odd modular weight (k=n+2),
this supplies the cohomological estimate needed for the remaining level-four
MTT comparison; the cusp-form lower bound remains a separate input.

## Sources and verification

The transversal/Schreier organization adapts the platform's accepted
[level-three generator proof](p2m:solution/d00a3882-a8c7-5dbc-8397-bc8df7d69c69).
The new modulo-four calculation is not a field argument, and its identities
are checked independently. Schreier's lemma is Mathlib's
`Subgroup.closure_mul_image_eq` in `Mathlib/GroupTheory/Schreier.lean`.
The final dimension shift is the proved
[normalization-kernel theorem](p2m:theorem/3352ccf6-5b8e-4aca-90ec-e016d14106d8).

The complete local development has only the standard Lean axioms. The
standalone submission imports that already-Proved normalization theorem
through its exact platform mirror, and no open theorem. It uses default
heartbeat and recursion limits, with no `sorry`, custom axiom or
`native_decide` in the submitted proof. No AINTLIB code was needed here.

